# OpenSpec Change Artifacts

OpenSpec records a proposed change through a small set of structured artifacts.
Use `openspec status --change <id> --json` to locate the artifacts for a
change. Their locations are resolved by the CLI and must not be inferred from
the current worktree.

Before authoring an artifact, run `openspec instructions <artifact> --change
<id> --json`. Those schema-specific instructions are authoritative for current
dependencies, format, and validation rules.

```bash
openspec instructions proposal --change <id> --json
openspec instructions design --change <id> --json
openspec instructions specs --change <id> --json
openspec instructions tasks --change <id> --json
```

## `proposal.md` — why and scope

The proposal establishes why the change is needed and what it intends to
change. For a proposal-led change it must identify the problem or opportunity,
the intended outcome, scope and non-goals, affected capabilities, and impact
on code, users, or compatibility.

It is not an implementation design or a task list. Direct work that restores
established behaviour, corrects a typo, changes formatting, or is otherwise
routine and non-behavioural does not require a proposal.

## `design.md` — technical decisions

`design.md` is optional. Create it when the change needs a recorded technical
decision: an architectural choice, alternatives with meaningful trade-offs,
cross-cutting behaviour, significant dependency or data-model changes,
security, performance, or migration risks, or an open technical question.

Its format records context, goals and non-goals, decisions and rationale,
alternatives, risks or trade-offs, migration considerations, and open
questions. Omit it when the proposal makes the technical approach obvious.

## Delta `spec.md` files — normative behaviour

A delta spec defines a behaviour contract that is added, modified, removed, or
renamed. Create one only when the change alters user- or system-observable
behaviour. A change that preserves the existing contract, such as an internal
refactor or tooling-only work, does not need a delta spec. If the schema needs
an explicit no-spec marker, use its documented `skip_specs` configuration
rather than adding a fictitious requirement.

Each delta covers one capability. A change may contain multiple delta specs,
one for every affected capability; a delta may bundle multiple related
requirements for its own capability.

Use the operation headings `## ADDED Requirements`, `## MODIFIED
Requirements`, `## REMOVED Requirements`, and `## RENAMED Requirements` as
applicable. Requirements use `### Requirement: <name>` and each must include
at least one `#### Scenario: <name>` with explicit WHEN/THEN behaviour.
`MODIFIED` requirements must include the complete updated requirement block.

Keep every requirement description—the text between `### Requirement:` and its
first scenario—to 500 characters or fewer. Put examples and edge cases in
scenarios, and split independent behaviour into separate requirements. For a
new capability, include the required `## Purpose` section; do not add one to a
delta for an existing capability.

## `tasks.md` — implementation work

`tasks.md` is the ordered checklist of discrete implementation work required
to deliver an approved change. Use Markdown task items and mark an item
complete only after its work is complete.

Tasks record implementation work only. Routine testing, validation, linting,
review, CI, and similar delivery checks are not separate OpenSpec tasks.
